### How do you write a UnaryOperator for name normalisation?

UnaryOperator extends Function with both type parameters equal.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.UnaryOperator;

public class NormaliseName {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        UnaryOperator<String> normalise = s -> s.strip().toLowerCase();

        List<String> names = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            "  Rajesh ", "PRIYA", " amit  "));

        names.replaceAll(normalise);
        System.out.println(names);
    }
}
```

```text
[rajesh, priya, amit]
```

### Why use UnaryOperator instead of Function<String, String>?

UnaryOperator signals intent: the operation preserves the type. APIs like List.replaceAll specifically require UnaryOperator, not Function.

| Type | Signature | Use case |
|---|---|---|
| UnaryOperator<T> | T -> T | Type-preserving: normalise, encrypt |
| Function<T, R> | T -> R | Type-changing: Employee -> String |

### How do you chain normalisation steps?

Because UnaryOperator is a Function, it supports andThen and compose.

```java
UnaryOperator<String> stripOp = String::strip;
UnaryOperator<String> lowerOp = String::toLowerCase;

// Chain: strip then lowercase
UnaryOperator<String> normalise =
    ((UnaryOperator<String>) String::strip)
        .andThen(String::toLowerCase)::apply;

// Or simply:
UnaryOperator<String> simple = s -> s.strip().toLowerCase();
```

### Why prefer strip() over trim()?

| Method | Whitespace handled | Introduced |
|---|---|---|
| trim() | ASCII whitespace only (chars <= 0x20) | Java 1.0 |
| strip() | Unicode whitespace (Character.isWhitespace) | Java 11 |

```java
String s = "\u2003Rajesh\u2003";  // em space (Unicode)
System.out.println(s.trim());   // "\u2003Rajesh\u2003" (not trimmed!)
System.out.println(s.strip());  // "Rajesh" (trimmed correctly)
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake: using different normalisation in different code paths.

```java
// Path 1: lowercase only
String key1 = name.toLowerCase();

// Path 2: trim + lowercase
String key2 = name.strip().toLowerCase();

// These produce different keys for "  Rajesh " → phantom duplicates!
// CORRECT: use the same UnaryOperator everywhere
static final UnaryOperator<String> NORMALISE = s -> s.strip().toLowerCase();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- Keep normalisation in a single, well-named operator so every code path uses the same canonicalisation.
- Inconsistent normalisation causes phantom duplicates: two entries for the same person that look different.
- UnaryOperator.identity() returns the input unchanged, useful as a default.
- For streams, .map(normalise) works because UnaryOperator is a Function.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What is BinaryOperator?" A specialisation of BiFunction<T, T, T> where all types are the same.
- "Can UnaryOperator be used with mapToInt?" No; mapToInt requires ToIntFunction. Use IntUnaryOperator for primitive ints.
- "How do you normalise in a stream?" .map(normalise) applies the operator to each element.
